### 1. Modify object.h

Replace the character pointer with a flexible array member:

```c
struct ObjString {
  Obj obj;
  int length;
  char chars[];
};
```

This allows the structure and its character data to be stored
in one continuous block of memory.

### 2. Modify object.c

Create a function that allocates enough memory for the
ObjString structure and all of its characters:

```c
ObjString* makeString(int length) {
  ObjString* string = (ObjString*)allocateObject(
      sizeof(ObjString) + length + 1, OBJ_STRING);
  string->length = length;
  return string;
}

ObjString* copyString(const char* chars, int length) {
  ObjString* string = makeString(length);

  memcpy(string->chars, chars, length);
  string->chars[length] = '\0';

  return string;
}
```

The makeString() function allocates space for both the string
object and the characters, including the null terminator.

### 3. Modify vm.c

Update concatenation to write the result directly into
the flexible array:

```c
static void concatenate() {
  ObjString* b = AS_STRING(pop());
  ObjString* a = AS_STRING(pop());

  int length = a->length + b->length;
  ObjString* result = makeString(length);

  memcpy(result->chars, a->chars, a->length);
  memcpy(result->chars + a->length, b->chars, b->length);
  result->chars[length] = '\0';

  push(OBJ_VAL(result));
}
```

### 4. Modify memory.c

Free the string using a single memory deallocation:

```c
case OBJ_STRING: {
  ObjString* string = (ObjString*)object;
  reallocate(object,
      sizeof(ObjString) + string->length + 1, 0);
  break;
}
```
